18716577540107, "y0": 0.10909090909090909, "x1": 0.841711229946524, "y1": 0.1231404958677686}, {"text": "Universe cooled to 10\u201d K, quarks and electrons formed, protons and neutrons formed,", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.14297520661157026, "x1": 0.8877005347593583, "y1": 0.1578512396694215}, {"text": "a)", "x0": 0.15080213903743314, "y0": 0.1446280991735537, "x1": 0.16470588235294117, "y1": 0.15702479338842976}, {"text": "temperature cooled to 10 K, thermonuclear fusion began, temperature cooled to 3000 K", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.16115702479338842, "x1": 0.9112299465240642, "y1": 0.17520661157024794}, {"text": "and radiation was free to move through the universe.", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.1793388429752066, "x1": 0.6203208556149733, "y1": 0.1933884297520661}, {"text": "Universe cooled to 10 \u201d K, protons and neutrons formed, quarks and electrons formed, ,", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.21322314049586777, "x1": 0.8973262032085562, "y1": 0.228099173553719}, {"text": "b)", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.21487603305785125, "x1": 0.16684491978609625, "y1": 0.22727272727272727}, {"text": "temperature cooled to 10 K, thermonuclear fusion began, temperature cooled to 3000 K", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.23140495867768596, "x1": 0.9112299465240642, "y1": 0.24545454545454545}, {"text": "and radiation was free to move through the universe.", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.2487603305785124, "x1": 0.6203208556149733, "y1": 0.2628099173553719}, {"text": "Universe cooled to 10\u201d K, quarks and electrons formed, radiation was free to move", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.28264462809917357, "x1": 0.8663101604278075, "y1": 0.2975206611570248}, {"text": "through the universe, protons and neutrons formed, temperature cooled to 10 \" K,", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.3, "x1": 0.8491978609625669, "y1": 0.3148760330578512}, {"text": "thermonuclear fusion began, temperature cooled to 3000 K.", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.3181818181818182, "x1": 0.6748663101604279, "y1": 0.3322314049586777}, {"text": "Universe cooled to 10\u201d K, quarks and electrons formed, protons and neutrons formed,", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.35206611570247937, "x1": 0.8877005347593583, "y1": 0.3669421487603306}, {"text": "temperature cooled to 10\" K, thermonuclear fusion began, temperature cooled to 3000 K.", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.3702479338842975, "x1": 0.9197860962566845, "y1": 0.3851239669421488}, {"text": "Which of the following is a correct statement for the law of reflection?", "x0": 0.18181818181818182, "y0": 0.4231404958677686, "x1": 0.73475935828877, "y1": 0.4371900826446281}, {"text": "15.", "x0": 0.12513368983957218, "y0": 0.42396694214876035, "x1": 0.14545454545454545, "y1": 0.43388429752066116}, {"text": "the speed of a wave changes as it changes mediums, in proportion to the refractive index", "x0": 0.2106951871657754, "y0": 0.47520661157024796, "x1": 0.9037433155080213, "y1": 0.48925619834710743}, {"text": "a)", "x0": 0.18181818181818182, "y0": 0.47603305785123967, "x1": 0.19572192513368983, "y1": 0.4884297520661157}, {"text": "light waves will always totally internally reflect when the refractive index of the second", "x0": 0.2106951871657754, "y0": 0.5099173553719009, "x1": 0.8983957219251337, "y1": 0.5239669421487604}, {"text": "b)", "x0": 0.18074866310160428, "y0": 0.5107438016528926, "x1": 0.19679144385026737, "y1": 0.5231404958677686}, {"text": "material is lower than the first", "x0": 0.2106951871657754, "y0": 0.5272727272727272, "x1": 0.4449197860962567, "y1": 0.5380165289256198}, {"text": "the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection", "x0": 0.2106951871657754, "y0": 0.5619834710743802, "x1": 0.613903743315508, "y1": 0.5760330578512397}, {"text": "waves spread out as they travel through a narrow opening", "x0": 0.2106951871657754, "y0": 0.5975206611570248, "x1": 0.6620320855614973, "y1": 0.6115702479338843}, {"text": "d)", "x0": 0.18074866310160428, "y0": 0.5983471074380166, "x1": 0.19679144385026737, "y1": 0.6107438016528925}, {"text": "Year 11 2unitPhyses 2003 page: 5", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.9413223140495868, "x1": 0.7700534759358288, "y1": 0.9545454545454546}], [{"text": "PARTB Total marks: 50. Allow about | hour and 40 minutes for this part", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.10909090909090909, "x1": 0.8192513368983957, "y1": 0.1231404958677686}, {"text": "Answer questions 16 \u2014 29 in the answer space. Show all relevant working and calculations.", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.1446280991735537, "x1": 0.8171122994652407, "y1": 0.1578512396694215}, {"text": "16. Circuit breakers and fuses are used to protect humans in their homes from the danger of electric", "x0": 0.0962566844919786, "y0": 0.1933884297520661, "x1": 0.8994652406417112, "y1": 0.2140495867768595}, {"text": "shock.", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.21487603305785125, "x1": 0.2, "y1": 0.22479338842975208}, {"text": "a) is one advantage that a circuit breaker has over a fuse? (1 mark )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.2413223140495868, "x1": 0.9080213903743316, "y1": 0.2652892561983471}, {"text": "b) _ Discuss the different effect AC power and DC power has on the muscles of the body.", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.27603305785123966, "x1": 0.8770053475935828, "y1": 0.30247933884297523}, {"text": "(2 marks )", "x0": 0.8235294117647058, "y0": 0.30165289256198347, "x1": 0.9080213903743316, "y1": 0.3140495867768595}, {"text": "17. a) Onthe diagram in the answer space", "x0": 0.0962566844919786, "y0": 0.3528925619834711, "x1": 0.48770053475935826, "y1": 0.3669421487603306}, {"text": "sketch the electric field lines between", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.37107438016528926, "x1": 0.5016042780748663, "y1": 0.38181818181818183}, {"text": "the plates. (1 mark ) 2", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.38925619834710745, "x1": 0.8673796791443851, "y1": 0.40743801652892564}, {"text": "b) On the diagram in the answer space,", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.5099173553719009, "x1": 0.4641711229946524, "y1": 0.5239669421487604}, {"text": "sketch the magnetic field lines", "x0": 0.18181818181818182, "y0": 0.5272727272727272, "x1": 0.41818181818181815, "y1": 0.5413223140495868}, {"text": "between the magnets.", "x0": 0.18074866310160428, "y0": 0.5454545454545454, "x1": 0.34866310160427805, "y1": 0.5586776859504132}, {"text": "(1 mark )", "x0": 0.3390374331550802, "y0": 0.5628099173553719, "x1": 0.41497326203208557, "y1": 0.5752066115702479}, {"text": "c) one way in which magnetic fields or magnets are used around the home. (I mark )", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.6495867768595042, "x1": 0.8983957219251337, "y1": 0.6636363636363637}, {"text": "Using coal as an energy source has changed in the last two hundred years. Coal was originally", "x0": 0.15294117647058825, "y0": 0.7363636363636363, "x1": 0.8994652406417112, "y1": 0.7504132231404959}, {"text": "used in fires to heat the home. Give two ways in which this has changed in the last 200 years.", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.7537190082644628, "x1": 0.893048128342246, "y1": 0.7677685950413223}, {"text": "(2 marks )", "x0": 0.8096256684491978, "y0": 0.7727272727272727, "x1": 0.893048128342246, "y1": 0.7851239669421488}, {"text": "19. heater rated at 2400 watts is used for 10 hours. If electricity costs 25 cents per", "x0": 0.0962566844919786, "y0": 0.8380165289256198, "x1": 0.8631016042780749, "y1": 0.8586776859504133}, {"text": "kilowatt-hour, how much will this (2 marks )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.8586776859504133, "x1": 0.9026737967914439, "y1": 0.871900826446281}, {"text": "Year 11 2unitPhyses 2003 page: 6", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.9413223140495868, "x1": 0.7700534759358288, "y1": 0.9545454545454546}], [{"text": "20. A student sets up this circuit. The voltage of the battery is", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.07107438016528926, "x1": 0.6128342245989304, "y1": 0.09173553719008265}, {"text": "12 volts and when the switch $1 is closed, a current of 3", "x0": 0.15508021390374332, "y0": 0.0884297520661157, "x1": 0.5957219251336898, "y1": 0.10991735537190082}, {"text": "amps flow around the circuit. The student notes the", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.10909090909090909, "x1": 0.5668449197860963, "y1": 0.1231404958677686}, {"text": "brightness of lamp LI.", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.12644628099173555, "x1": 0.32727272727272727, "y1": 0.14049586776859505}, {"text": "a) Calculate the resistance of lamp LI. (1 mark )", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.1537190082644628, "x1": 0.5272727272727272, "y1": 0.17851239669421487}, {"text": "b) Calculate the electrical power used by lamp L1.( 1)", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.19669421487603306, "x1": 0.558288770053476, "y1": 0.21074380165289255}, {"text": "The student then changes the circuit. ?", "x0": 0.11229946524064172, "y0": 0.30082644628099175, "x1": 0.8481283422459893, "y1": 0.3148760330578512}, {"text": "All lamps are identical to the lamp L1.", "x0": 0.11336898395721925, "y0": 0.33553719008264465, "x1": 0.413903743315508, "y1": 0.3495867768595041}, {"text": "St", "x0": 0.5197860962566845, "y0": 0.3975206611570248, "x1": 0.5433155080213904, "y1": 0.4190082644628099}, {"text": "\u00a9) Switch S1 and S2 are closed. Compare the brightness of each lamp to the brightness of L1 in", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.44049586776859506, "x1": 0.8866310160427807, "y1": 0.45454545454545453}, {"text": "the original circuit. (dimmer, same or brighter ) ? (3 marks )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.45041322314049587, "x1": 0.8909090909090909, "y1": 0.47520661157024796}, {"text": "21. Identify the difference between conductors and insulators. (2 marks )", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.5099173553719009, "x1": 0.8877005347593583, "y1": 0.5239669421487604}, {"text": "22. a) Sketch a Hertzsprung \u2014 Russell diagram and label the axes. (3 marks )", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.5628099173553719, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.5760330578512397}, {"text": "b) On your diagram indicate the regions of: (3 marks )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.5975206611570248, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.6115702479338843}, {"text": "i) main sequence stars", "x0": 0.22139037433155082, "y0": 0.6148760330578512, "x1": 0.42459893048128344, "y1": 0.6289256198347107}, {"text": "ii) red giants", "x0": 0.2160427807486631, "y0": 0.6322314049586777, "x1": 0.3443850267379679, "y1": 0.6462809917355372}, {"text": "iii) dwarfs", "x0": 0.2106951871657754, "y0": 0.6495867768595042, "x1": 0.3700534759358289, "y1": 0.6628099173553719}, {"text": "c) on your diagram indicate the position of our sun (G2) (1 mark )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.684297520661157, "x1": 0.8759358288770054, "y1": 0.6983471074380165}, {"text": "23. accretion and briefly show how it helps explain the formation of stars and galaxies.( 2 )", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.7363636363636363, "x1": 0.8941176470588236, "y1": 0.7504132231404959}, {"text": "24. a) List one of the two assumptions made by Friedmann about the universe. (1 mark )", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.7859504132231405, "x1": 0.8759358288770054, "y1": 0.8066115702479338}, {"text": "b) Friedmann proposed a model to explain the fate of our universe. This \u201cclosed universe", "x0": 0.15080213903743314, "y0": 0.8239669421487603, "x1": 0.8962566844919786, "y1": 0.8380165289256198}, {"text": "model\u201d is one of three proposed possible explanations. The other two are the \u201cflat", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.8413223140495868, "x1": 0.8588235294117647, "y1": 0.8553719008264463}, {"text": "universe model\u201d and the \u201copen universe model\u201d.", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.8586776859504133, "x1": 0.5882352941176471, "y1": 0.8727272727272727}, {"text": "Choose one of these models and explain how it works. (2 marks )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.8900826446280992, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.9107438016528926}, {"text": "Year 11 2unitPhyses 2003 page: 7", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.9413223140495868, "x1": 0.7700534759358288, "y1": 0.9545454545454546}], [{"text": "a) Briefly name or describe one form of evidence that supports the big bang theory.", "x0": 0.15080213903743314, "y0": 0.0743801652892562, "x1": 0.8406417112299466, "y1": 0.0884297520661157}, {"text": "25.", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.07520661157024794, "x1": 0.11550802139037433, "y1": 0.08512396694214876}, {"text": "Explain how this evidence supports the big bang theory (2 marks )", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.09173553719008265, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.10578512396694215}, {"text": "b) _ Briefly name or describe a second form of evidence that supports the big bang theory.", "x0": 0.15080213903743314, "y0": 0.11900826446280992, "x1": 0.8802139037433155, "y1": 0.14545454545454545}, {"text": "Explain how this evidence supports the big bang theory (2 marks )", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.14380165289256197, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.1578512396694215}, {"text": "a) A light ray travelling in a vacuum, has a wavelength of 5 x 107m. The ray enters a", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.18925619834710744, "x1": 0.8727272727272727, "y1": 0.21570247933884298}, {"text": "26.", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.1975206611570248, "x1": 0.11550802139037433, "y1": 0.20743801652892563}, {"text": "slab that has a refractive index of 1.5. Calculate the wavelength of the light in the", "x0": 0.253475935828877, "y0": 0.2140495867768595, "x1": 0.8994652406417112, "y1": 0.228099173553719}, {"text": "(2 marks )", "x0": 0.8, "y0": 0.2322314049586777, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.24462809917355371}, {"text": "b) Another light ray travelling in a vacuum, has a wavelength of 5 x 107m. The ray enters", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.25950413223140495, "x1": 0.9090909090909091, "y1": 0.28429752066115704}, {"text": "a perspex slab that has a refractive index of 1.3. Calculate the wavelength of the light in", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.2834710743801653, "x1": 0.9058823529411765, "y1": 0.2975206611570248}, {"text": "the perspex. (1 mark )", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.30165289256198347, "x1": 0.8759358288770054, "y1": 0.3148760330578512}, {"text": "c) Calculate the ratio of the speed of the light ray in the glass to the speed of the light ray in", "x0": 0.15080213903743314, "y0": 0.33553719008264465, "x1": 0.9037433155080213, "y1": 0.3495867768595041}, {"text": "the perspex. (1 marks )", "x0": 0.20962566844919786, "y0": 0.3537190082644628, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.3669421487603306}, {"text": "Modern communication methods rely on electromagnetic radiation and digital storage.", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.40578512396694216, "x1": 0.8299465240641711, "y1": 0.41983471074380163}, {"text": "27.", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.4066115702479339, "x1": 0.11550802139037433, "y1": 0.41652892561983473}, {"text": "a) Identify two communication wavebands within the electromagnetic spectrum. ( 2 marks)", "x0": 0.15080213903743314, "y0": 0.44049586776859506, "x1": 0.9037433155080213, "y1": 0.45454545454545453}, {"text": "b) Identify two types of communication data that are stored in digital form. (2 marks )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.47520661157024796, "x1": 0.8834224598930481, "y1": 0.48925619834710743}, {"text": "A ray of light, travelling in air, is incident on a horizontal glass surface at an angle of incidence", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.5272727272727272, "x1": 0.8983957219251337, "y1": 0.5413223140495868}, {"text": "28.", "x0": 0.09304812834224599, "y0": 0.528099173553719, "x1": 0.11550802139037433, "y1": 0.5380165289256198}, {"text": "of 30 degrees. If the refractive index of the glass is 1.5, find the angle of refraction of the light", "x0": 0.1518716577540107, "y0": 0.5371900826446281, "x1": 0.8983957219251337, "y1": 0.5636363636363636}, {"text": "ray in the glas (2 marks )", "x0": 0.1518716577540107,